# A generator that generates itself

Literate's book describes the engine that reads Literate's book. That statement is
appealing, but it contains a circle. Before the described engine exists, something
must already be able to read the description and produce executable source. After
a change, the project also needs evidence that the produced engine agrees with the
book that produced it.

Self-hosting manages this circle with explicit bootstrap material and a fixed-point
check. It does not ask the circle to justify itself from nothing.

## Bootstrap material makes the first step possible

A checkout carries generated source and a known executable path capable of reading
the authoritative literate documents. These files are not a second authority. They
are the material needed to turn the authority—the book—into a fresh executable.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Book[Authoritative literate project] --> Bootstrap[Known generator]
    Bootstrap --> Plan[Fresh generated sources]
    Plan --> Build[Fresh executable engine]
    Build --> Again[Generate from the same book]
    Again --> Compare[Compare complete plans]
```

The first arrow means “is read by,” not “is derived from.” That asymmetry is the
bootstrap assumption. The remaining arrows test whether the assumption leads to a
self-consistent generator.

Generated source remains useful even though it is not authoritative. It lets common
compilers and build tools construct the generator without first understanding
literate syntax. It also gives reviewers a conventional representation of the
program. Any direct edit to that representation is temporary, however, because the
next generation derives it again from the book.

## A generator language must be able to describe itself

Self-description creates a lexical problem before it creates a philosophical one.
The generator's source needs to discuss and recognize fragment delimiters that are
also meaningful to the generator. Literal delimiter escaping keeps that description
inside the ordinary language instead of granting the self-hosted project a hidden
exception.

An illustration can show the syntax without defining a live fragment:

```text
source : \<<generator entry.*>>= generator.ts $
    \<<parse fragment declarations>>
```

The escaped opening delimiters are literal text in this chapter. During normal
source expansion an escaped delimiter becomes an unescaped delimiter exactly once.
The produced text is not rescanned as another layer of fragment instructions. A
single, explicit escape rule is easier to reason about than special cases activated
only while generating the generator.

## Bootstrap readability is an invariant

The authoritative book must remain readable by an available bootstrap generator
until a fresh generator can reproduce it. A language change may require a temporary
compatibility bridge, but the enduring rule is simple: never make the only available
generator unable to read the book that defines its replacement.

The same invariant covers deliberate output-shape changes. Compilation alone is not
enough; the fresh engine must interpret the captured input and reach the exact plan
that will serve as the next bootstrap state.

## The fixed point is an equality of plans

Let `S` be the complete captured literate snapshot. Let `P` be the complete output
plan produced from `S` by the trusted bootstrap path. Build a fresh engine `E` from
the generated engine source contained in `P`. Self-hosting reaches a fixed point
when:

```text
E(S) = P
```

The equality covers the set of output paths and the exact bytes at every path.
Array order is irrelevant because a plan is identified by its destinations, but a
missing path, an extra path, or one changed byte breaks the equality.

In ordinary language, the newly described generator must read the same book and
produce exactly the material from which it was built. The formula is useful because
it rules out weaker interpretations such as “the important file looks similar” or
“running the new engine did not throw an error.”

## Verify fresh source without replacing the checkout

Fixed-point verification should not depend on writing fresh generated files over the
bootstrap files it is trying to check. Instead, it creates an in-memory overlay: a
map in which generated paths resolve to fresh planned content while ordinary
handwritten sources and installed dependencies still resolve from the checkout.

The overlay boundary is validated first. Destinations must remain beneath the
project root, duplicates are rejected, and the fresh engine and command-line source
must both be present. Generated source directories are represented virtually so
module resolution can find files that need not exist on disk.

A compiler then checks one program built from the overlay and the project's ordinary
configuration. Emission and incremental state are disabled. Existing handwritten
sources and tests remain part of the type-check, while stale generated source on
disk cannot take precedence over fresh planned content.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Plan[Fresh complete plan] --> Layout[Validate overlay layout]
    Layout --> Types[Type-check fresh sources in memory]
    Types --> Bundle[Bundle the fresh engine from the overlay]
    Bundle --> Child[Run the fresh engine in a bounded child]
    Child --> Result{Exact plan equality?}
    Result -->|Yes| Fixed[Fixed point verified]
    Result -->|No| Fail[Named verification failure]
```

Each downward arrow is a policy boundary. A layout failure does not become a
compiler message. A type error prevents bundling. A bundling failure prevents
execution. A generator diagnostic or malformed child result prevents comparison.
Named stages make failure actionable without pretending that later evidence exists.

## Execute the fresh engine, not stale emitted code

Passing a type-check is necessary but not enough. The verifier bundles the engine
entry from overlay contents, resolving relative generated modules against the same
fresh map. Ordinary package resolution remains ordinary; a virtual directory must
not accidentally hide an existing sibling file or package entry.

The bundle runs in a separate child process with a private temporary working
directory. The original captured documents and templates are sent as data. The
child returns the generation result as data. A process boundary allows the verifier
to terminate a fresh engine that enters a synchronous infinite loop—something a
promise timeout in the parent could not interrupt.

The private directory limits accidental output in the checkout and makes cleanup
straightforward. It is not a security sandbox. The bundled engine is project-authored
code and may use the capabilities of the account that runs it. Self-verification is
therefore appropriate only for trusted source.

## Compare a complete and well-formed result

Before equality is tested, the child result must itself be credible. It must contain
an output list and a diagnostic list. Any generator diagnostic fails verification.
Every output must have a string path, string content, and a unique destination.
Malformed protocol data cannot count as an empty successful plan.

Comparison builds a map from regenerated destinations. Each planned path must occur
with exactly equal content and is removed as it matches. A missing path or content
difference fails immediately; entries left at the end are unexpected outputs. This
makes the comparison independent of incidental array ordering while preserving
exact path and byte identity.

Temporary material is removed on success and failure. Cleanup is part of the
verification result: a verifier that leaves unexplained executable material behind
has not completed all of its promised work.

`self-host compare output set` states fixed-point equality directly.

```typescript: <<self-host compare output set>>=
for (const file of files) {
    if (!actual.has(file.path)) { throw new Error(`Missing regenerated output: ${file.path}`); }
    if (actual.get(file.path) !== file.content) { throw new Error(`Regenerated content mismatch: ${file.path}`); }
    actual.delete(file.path);
}
if (actual.size) { throw new Error(`Unexpected regenerated output: ${[...actual.keys()].sort()[0]}`); }
```

## What the fixed point proves

A successful check provides strong, specific evidence:

- fresh generated sources satisfy the project's type constraints together with the
  surrounding handwritten program;
- the engine that is executed was built from those fresh sources rather than stale
  emitted code;
- that engine accepts the captured authoritative project without diagnostics;
- it reproduces the same complete output path set and exact contents;
- verification did not need to replace project files.

This establishes self-consistency for the engine and captured project under the
verification environment. It also checks that fresh command-line source participates
in the type-correct program, even though the fixed-point execution itself invokes
the engine.

## What the fixed point cannot prove

Self-consistency is not the same as correctness. Two generations can agree on the
same wrong interpretation. Exact reproduction does not prove that diagnostics are
helpful, the command line reports failures correctly, output rollback works, an
editor handles stale buffers safely, or publication renders every supported construct
well. Those claims require focused tests and review at their own boundaries.

The check also does not make project code safe to execute, prove behavior on every
host filesystem, or establish crash durability. Its child timeout bounds engine
execution, not every preceding compiler or bundler operation. It verifies one
captured project; another snapshot must earn its own result.

These limitations do not weaken the fixed point. They locate it. Self-hosting answers
one difficult question precisely: does the freshly generated engine reproduce the
complete plan from which it was built when given the same authority?

With that circle made explicit, the book can turn to the editor without giving the
editor semantic authority of its own. The next chapter develops the editor-side
project repository: the long-lived host that captures workspace snapshots, gives
open buffers precedence, schedules refreshes, and keeps diagnostics and generated
effects current without confusing either task with parsing.
